  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">NGOF (SCOPE-PFM)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">NGOF is to be &#x2018;a professional membership-based organization influencing and supporting policies, laws development and practices that champion and foster people harmonization, inclusive prosperity, and a sustainable planet for the benefit of all diverse Cambodians. It&apos;s strategic goal aims to champion genuine multi-stakeholder engagement for transformative society, inclusive and green growth, a resilient and sustainable planet which benefits all, especially youth, women, indigenous, and marginalized people in Cambodia.

The NGOF specialises in evidence-based research on PFM, particularly in national budget analysis andcitizen budgeting. NGOF, in collaboration with the International Budget Partnership (IBP), conducts thebiennial Open Budget Survey (OBS) to enhance budget transparency and oversight. This initiative increases public access to timely, comprehensive, and clear budget information, aligning with the reform objectives of the EU budget support operation. They lead the BWG, facilitating discussions and advocacy on budget transparencyand PFM reforms. NGOF develops position papers and policy recommendations for key stakeholders such as the MEF and National Assembly. They are instrumental in creating platforms for dialogue among CSOs, government, and international partners. NGOF, together with API and TIC, representsthe BWG as observers in the Public Financial Management Reform Program Technical Working Group of the government. NGOF also provides training to build the capacity of its members and partners, ensuring effective advocacy. Their communicationefforts amplify CSO voices through media engagement, promoting the PFM reform agenda.

For the SCOPE-PFM project, the NGOF will contribute mainly on specific objective 1 is to increase the capacity and coordination of CSOs and their networks in advocating for budget transparency, accountability, and oversight in education, social protection,public services, and natural resources through jointinitiatives and targeted capacity-building activities.</narrative>
  </description>
  <participating-org role="4" type="24">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">NGO Forum on Cambodia</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The NGO Forum was started in Cambodia in the 1980s by international NGOs who wanted to put an end to Cambodia&apos;s international isolation and aid embargo. In the 1990s, as Cambodia opened up to the world, the NGO Forum became Cambodia-based, and began to invite local organisations to join as members. Nevertheless, it was still governed by an international Steering Committee and dominated by international NGOs right up to 1996. In 1997 a clear decision was made to make the NGO Forum more relevant to the concerns of the Cambodian people, resulting in an expansion of the membership base among local NGOs. A new expatriate representative was recruited and given the mandate to transition the NGO Forum towards full Cambodian leadership. Cambodian staff members began to play dominant roles in NGO Forum activities, and the first fully Cambodian Management Committee was elected in 2000. Over the course of the next eight years, the NGO Forum grew to respond to a number of emerging critical issues affecting poor and vulnerable communities across Cambodia.

The NGO Forum on Cambodia is a membership organisation for local and international NGOs based in Cambodia. It exists for the purpose of information sharing, debate and advocacy on priority issues affecting Cambodia&apos;s development. It aims to represent the viewsof the NGO community to decision-makers on issues such as development policy (overall economic and political issues), gender mainstreaming, and environmental protection and land rights. Presently the NGO Forum on Cambodia registers 79 full members and two associate members. Of the 79 full members, 46 are local NGOs and 33 are international NGOs.</narrative>
  </participating-org>
